WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.710
- Good afternoon everybody.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.150
You are very welcome here.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:13.320
I'm Steph Newell from the
English Department here at Yale.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.050
It's my greatest pleasure to introduce

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:17.730
our distinguished speaker this evening,

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.180
the Nobel Laureate
Professor Abdulrazak Gurnah.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.770
Let me start with some thanks

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.930
to our sponsors and organizers.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.570
Professor Gurnah's visit to
Yale has been made possible

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:30.660
by generous funding from the
Council on African Studies

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.490
at the Yale MacMillan Center

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.070
as well as the Trumbull
Endowed Lecture Fund,

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.840
the Whitney Humanities Center,

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.000
the office of the Dean of Humanities

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.957
and the English Department.

00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:43.230
Meanwhile, over several months

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.500
and with meticulous attention
to detail, Cristin Siebert

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.370
and Mariwa Kabor of the
Council on African Studies

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.220
and the Yale MacMillan Center
have organized everything

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.610
for Professor Gurnah's visit
from transportation to venues

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.920
to food to drink to much, much more.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.757
So Cristin and Mariwa wherever you are,

00:01:01.757 --> 00:01:03.600
there they are at the back,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.716
please, let's say thank you to them

00:01:05.716 --> 00:01:07.058
for all of their organizations.

00:01:07.058 --> 00:01:10.225
(audience applauding)

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.740
So our preparations have been supported

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.670
all along the way

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.980
by the Director of the
Whitney Humanities Center,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.480
Professor Alice Kaplan

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.360
and the Associate Director
Diane Barrett Brown.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.850
And thanks also go to the chair
of the English department,

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.910
Mark Robinson, the Chair of
the Council on African Studies,

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.380
Professor Cajetan Iheka,

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.620
and the Dean of Humanities,
Professor Katie Laughton.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.880
And they've all been very supportive,

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.770
and we are also grateful to Jen Castellon

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.620
and Michael Morand at
the Beinecke Library,

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:40.860
and you are all very warmly
invited after this lecture

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.043
to the Beinecke Library for a
reception and a musical treat.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.130
We're also very grateful
to the student helpers

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.810
who are here to help out this afternoon.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.680
So now, as you may have
gathered as you came in,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.240
your presence at this venue

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.630
is an indication of your
consent to the recording

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.610
that we are making of
Professor Gurnah's lecture.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.460
So we'll have time for
a short Q&amp;A after this.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.010
But by putting your hands up
and receiving the microphone,

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:08.130
you are also implicitly agreeing,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.663
consenting to being recorded.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.810
We've also got a book stall outside

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.250
which you may have seen on your way in.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.410
You're very welcome to buy copies of books

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.910
and Professor Gurnah has very
kindly agreed to sign books

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.703
after the lecture for a
very short period of time.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.120
So welcome, welcome to you.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.840
And now when Abdulrazak
Gurnah was named winner

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.750
of the Nobel Prize for literature in 2021,

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.820
his publishers were caught off guard

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.520
by the overnight upsurge
in demand of his novels.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.120
And for a brief period as
supply caught up with demand,

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.510
printed copies of his works
vanished from the shelves.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.790
Bookshops struggled to restock,

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.280
and the New York Times
complained in a headline

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.300
he won the Nobel.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.523
Why are his books so hard to find?

00:02:54.429 --> 00:02:55.262
(audience laughing)

00:02:55.262 --> 00:02:57.780
Meanwhile, the reaction
of literary scholars

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.592
to Professor Gurnah's Nobel
Prize was a noisy, "Of course!"

00:03:00.592 --> 00:03:05.250
As the Sudanese British
author Leila Aboulela put it,

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:08.877
she says "Now our well kept
secret is out in the open."

00:03:10.436 --> 00:03:11.269
For more than three decades,

00:03:11.269 --> 00:03:13.140
Professor Gurnah has been
an influential figure

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.510
among post-colonial literary scholars.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:18.990
He's the author of 10 novels,
numerous short stories,

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:23.370
academic books, reflective
essays and scholarly articles.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:24.780
And as Professor of Literature

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.270
at the University of Canterbury,

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.100
his work has helped to shape our field.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.550
So when literary scholars think
about the oceanic histories

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.920
of East Africa, for
example, and those old,

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.040
the old Spice routes
connecting Zanzibar with India

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.270
and the Arab world

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:40.950
or when they think about the experiences

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.990
of different kinds of migrants from Africa

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.903
to the global north,
they turn to his fiction.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.867
From the outset with the
publication of his first novel

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:52.500
"Memory of Departure" in 1987

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.900
Professor Gurnah has
focused on ordinary people

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.810
caught up in momentous
events in East Africa.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:00.330
So they're often set in
Zanzibar where he was born

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:03.840
but also in the neighboring
countries of Tanzania and Kenya.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.780
His novels feature characters
whose lives are impacted

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.900
by war, colonialism, decolonization,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.610
but often who have little control

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.430
over the events that shape their world.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.370
What horizons open and close, he asks,

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:18.990
in the lives of ordinary Africans,

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:19.823
when they coincide

00:04:19.823 --> 00:04:22.110
with these seismic
political transformations

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.720
on the continent that
are beyond their control.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.820
How do they navigate
through violent events

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.750
like European colonialism,
world wars, fascism,

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:34.460
displacement, revolutionary
post-colonial conflict?

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:36.780
In his Nobel Prize lecture,

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:39.150
Professor Gurnah describes
his creative writing

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.030
as retrieving stories, and again and again

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.150
you can see this process of
retrieval in his fiction.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.250
Whether it's an old man on his deathbed

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.620
with a secret to disclose to his family

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.330
as in "The Last Gift"

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.700
or a young man who
escapes domestic slavery

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.770
into another form of servitude

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:59.520
in the East African German
army in the First World War

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.280
as that's in his most
recent novel "Afterlives,"

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.780
his work focuses on the figures

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.360
whose experiences and
feelings are either absent

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:09.810
from the written archives
or so thoroughly overwritten

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.910
by colonial racial categories

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:14.820
that their subjective
experiences can't be recomposed

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.293
from the historical record.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.567
As the narrator puts it in "Afterlives,"

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:21.967
"East Africa has simply been a sideshow

00:05:21.967 --> 00:05:23.787
"to the great tragedies in Europe."

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:26.820
In Professor Gurnah's fiction

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:30.030
you do not find a comfortable
retrieval of stories

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.290
into visibility.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.960
Many of his characters
experience false memories

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.120
or sometimes their lies take on substance

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.280
and become part of their family history.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.544
Or new memories displace old ones

00:05:40.544 --> 00:05:43.710
or stories become riddled with doubt,

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.990
but complicated as the
stories and memories

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:47.280
of his characters are,

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.440
as their lives unfold in each novel,

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.920
we see tangled accounts
giving way to confessions,

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.160
confusion and evasion
giving way to clarification.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.580
Time and again in his novels,
histories are corrected.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.143
Memories are set right.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.110
While you find many
different kinds of narrator

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:05.670
in Professor Gurnah's novels,

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.710
but you also find many different scenes

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:10.650
of listening and examples of listeners

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.990
as stories are told and retold.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.060
The way people and their
stories are received

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.100
is vital to his work.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.607
A failure to listen is shown
to be another form of violence.

00:06:21.607 --> 00:06:24.180
"You cannot imagine what
that time was like,"

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.030
says the mother of the
narrator of "Gravel Heart"

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:28.140
as she tries yet again

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.560
to describe the trauma of
the January 1964 revolution

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:32.617
in Zanzibar.

00:06:32.617 --> 00:06:34.777
"You cannot imagine the terror of it,

00:06:34.777 --> 00:06:37.987
"the arrests, the
deaths, the humiliations.

00:06:37.987 --> 00:06:39.457
"People were driving each other mad

00:06:39.457 --> 00:06:41.682
"with rumors of new outrages, new decrees

00:06:41.682 --> 00:06:44.047
"with news of further sorrows.

00:06:44.047 --> 00:06:47.287
"But yes, you can imagine you must try.

00:06:47.287 --> 00:06:50.527
"Nothing stands between us
and atrocities but words.

00:06:50.527 --> 00:06:53.433
"So there is no choice
but to try and imagine.

00:06:54.967 --> 00:06:56.520
"If you stop trying to tell your story,"

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.220
she continues, "after a while,

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:01.957
"it becomes as if these
things did not really happen,

00:07:01.957 --> 00:07:03.247
"did not really happen like that,

00:07:03.247 --> 00:07:06.277
"as if you're exaggerating
if you speak of them.

00:07:06.277 --> 00:07:08.257
"So you stop speaking,

00:07:08.257 --> 00:07:09.937
"and they recede even further away.

00:07:09.937 --> 00:07:11.647
"They become even more unreal

00:07:11.647 --> 00:07:14.527
"become even less possible to imagine,

00:07:14.527 --> 00:07:16.567
"and you tell yourself
it's time to move on.

00:07:16.567 --> 00:07:17.400
"Let them go.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.197
"It's not worth the bother of remembering.

00:07:20.197 --> 00:07:22.107
"But they do not let you go."

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.920
Like the listeners inside his
stories, Abdulrazak Gurnah

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.160
calls on us as readers,
as listeners to imagine

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:31.620
and in so doing to inhabit and remember

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:33.600
the lives of his characters.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.090
Tonight's lecture is
called "Writing Memory."

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.919
Please welcome Professor
Abdulrazak Gurnah.

00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:42.086
(audience applauding)

00:07:54.337 --> 00:07:55.170
- Thank you very much.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:56.250
Thank you, Steph.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:57.744
Thank you.

00:07:57.744 --> 00:07:59.040
That was a wonderful introduction.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.720
I couldn't have asked you better,

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:02.370
and thank you all for being here.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:04.890
I'm very happy to be here

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:06.570
and to answer the question

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:10.860
that I'm sure you're burning
to get an answer to is

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:13.413
yes, it's the first
time I'm coming to Yale.

00:08:14.897 --> 00:08:15.960
(audience laughing)

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.490
But it seems to be wherever you go,

00:08:17.490 --> 00:08:19.320
people say is this the
first time you're here?

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.000
So you know, I like to
get this out of the way

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.380
as soon as possible.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:23.370
So yes it is.

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.241
Yes it's good to be here.

00:08:26.241 --> 00:08:31.241
It's been relatively
nice. (audience laughing)

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:36.453
A lot nicer than where
we've come from in the UK.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:39.660
Anyway, really Steph, thank you very much

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:41.973
for that introduction, that appreciation.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.663
I've already met some of you.

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.330
I met some of you yesterday.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:50.040
Some of the students yesterday,

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.010
and I know and have
heard already from you.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.303
So glad to see you again.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.470
Okay, so it's always interesting

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.740
when you are invited to give a lecture,

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.980
say a year before the
lecture is due to be given.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.846
And so we said, all right, what
are you going to talk about?

00:09:07.846 --> 00:09:11.400
And you know, nothing's
further away from your thoughts

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:14.013
than this event that's
gonna happen a year hence.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:19.970
But I knew that this idea,
this topic of memory,

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:23.640
this, it's something
that I think about a lot

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.010
and talk about and write about.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:28.064
So I thought this would be something

00:09:28.064 --> 00:09:32.820
that it might be interesting
to put some ideas together

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:34.863
and see what comes out.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:41.630
So for a writer or at least
for a writer of a certain kind,

00:09:43.262 --> 00:09:45.090
a writer like, I mean in that respect,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:50.070
not the person, but the
kind of writing I do.

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.460
So for a writer of that kind,

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:57.693
the kind I'm familiar with,
memory is a vital resource.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.790
I'm not referring to
the ability to remember

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:07.790
when I say memory, to remember
certain facts or details,

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:10.800
but I'm referring to the swamp of memories

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.960
that linger on long after
the moment has passed

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.600
and which at times strike out of silence

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.973
to leave one quivering with sorrow,

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:25.653
and if one is lucky
with unexpected elation.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:29.580
I think of this idea of memory,

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:32.350
this idea of memory is not facts

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:34.950
or not remembered things exactly,

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:38.250
but this kind of, I call it
swamp, but you know what I mean.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:42.540
This kind of indefinable thing
that lurks all the time there

00:10:42.540 --> 00:10:44.990
and every now and again
surfaces and catches you.

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:47.490
I think of this idea of memory

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:51.366
as a writer's hinterland
in the sense that,

00:10:51.366 --> 00:10:55.200
hinterland in the sense
that large urban centers

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.600
depend on the country around them

00:10:57.600 --> 00:10:59.703
to provide the means to sustain them.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.050
I used this word to describe memory,

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:06.930
and somebody says why do you
want to use a German word?

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:08.460
I said, no, no, no.

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:09.293
Hinterland is not a German word.

00:11:09.293 --> 00:11:12.083
Didn't you do all level
geography or something like that.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:15.993
But another way of speaking,

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:18.553
the hinterland in the sense of

00:11:19.395 --> 00:11:20.513
it provides the source material,

00:11:21.519 --> 00:11:23.219
the place where the
writer goes back to for,

00:11:23.219 --> 00:11:26.310
to kind of check the plausibility

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:29.013
of certain notions and ideas
and that sort of thing.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:31.710
But another way of speaking about memory

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.293
is to describe it as experience.

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:39.030
We remember what we have experienced,

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.813
and we do so in a profound
sense of that word experience.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:45.693
Now there may be events,

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:51.300
experience may be events
which befell us in person

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:55.710
or stories we heard about
other people in other places

00:11:55.710 --> 00:12:00.120
or news or histories we read about

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:04.980
or dreams and imaginings
that thrilled, excuse me,

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:06.663
that thrilled or baffled us.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:10.470
I think of experience
as all of those things.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:13.293
This is what goes on in our consciousness.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:17.070
Both because we live it,
because we hear about it,

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:20.460
because we read about it or
in other ways we imagine it.

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:22.910
We anticipate it because
of what we already know.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:29.520
It's the writer's self-inflicted burden

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:34.230
to make something engaging,
productive, or even beautiful

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:35.283
out of this.

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:43.990
The recall of memory in writing

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:47.220
performs several of these functions

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:49.500
that I've been touching on.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:54.150
It engages the recording of
memory in writing engages

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:57.243
by evoking salient details.

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:01.080
It makes the engagement productive

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:02.643
because we believe in it.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:08.460
We find it plausible
and we hear echos of it

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:09.840
in our own knowledge.

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:11.580
There's all this lovely moment

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:13.030
when you're reading something

00:13:14.465 --> 00:13:15.560
and you're kind of without being aware,

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:17.001
and you're nodding because you think

00:13:17.001 --> 00:13:18.016
yes, yes, yes, yes, yes

00:13:18.016 --> 00:13:20.460
because we're hearing something
that is both persuasive

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:22.623
but also because we recognize something.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:25.830
And if the writer has been blessed

00:13:25.830 --> 00:13:29.310
with the ability and insight,
the product is even beautiful.

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:32.340
So you not only nod and say that,

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:34.140
but you think, oh gosh, that's good.

00:13:35.061 --> 00:13:36.646
How did he do that?

00:13:36.646 --> 00:13:38.970
(audience laughing)

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:43.293
We read for comparably complex ends.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:47.790
There is pleasure in
the engagement itself.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:51.100
I would've thought that's
probably the primary part

00:13:51.100 --> 00:13:53.310
of the experience of reading.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.110
It doesn't work if there isn't that.

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:59.070
So there is pleasure in the engagement

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:02.820
which is difficult to describe
but which is palpable,

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:06.933
which is something we
can feel when we feel it.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:11.640
That pleasure it seems to
me is as I just mentioned,

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.980
primary, unless of course

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:17.875
if we're in the midst of our
university literature program.

00:14:17.875 --> 00:14:20.490
(audience laughing)

00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:24.810
We see our inclinations
and ideas expressed

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:28.833
more persuasively than we are
capable of doing ourselves.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:34.620
We're exposed to places and
times and ways of thinking,

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:38.043
some of which are familiar and
some completely unfamiliar.

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:42.330
In other words, in addition
to the pleasure of reading

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:46.770
something beautifully
or powerfully expressed,

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:49.080
we also receive news.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:49.913
We learn.

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:56.790
The least of these functions

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:59.730
that I've been trying to
summarize is if you like,

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:03.540
why do we read or what
do we get outta reading?

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:05.253
The list of these functions,

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:10.290
which the recall of
memory in writing performs

00:15:10.290 --> 00:15:14.043
is to evoke nostalgia for a lost time,

00:15:17.190 --> 00:15:19.290
to speak a lament for a contentment

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:20.703
which is no longer possible.

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:26.220
Nostalgia in this sense
would seem to refer

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:31.220
to a kind of self-deception,
an introspective desire

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:35.220
to see what is passed more positively

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:36.783
than the present state.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:42.660
And as scholars and
students of literature,

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:45.063
we are often skeptical of such tropes.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.603
Indeed, we are trained to be skeptical,

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:52.180
and there are good reasons for this

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:58.730
both in reading literature
and in living our lives

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:04.113
because at worst, sentiment
can also be a means of evasion,

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:08.430
a refusal to acknowledge responsibility

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:10.113
for what was done in our name.

00:16:14.397 --> 00:16:16.950
On the other hand, let's not be in a hurry

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:19.413
to throw out this word nostalgia.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:26.853
You can find this out by
just checking on Google.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:30.360
The word nostalgia derives
from the Greek words

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:35.360
nostos, which means return
and algos, which means pain.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:40.530
And one of its meanings is the distress

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:44.553
aroused by a yearning for
one's place of origin.

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:52.344
It's a yearning that those who have lost

00:16:52.344 --> 00:16:56.550
their places of origin,
have lost their homes,

00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:57.843
know only too well.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:08.360
In recent times, we have witnessed
a strange panic in Europe

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:14.073
about the arrival of what
are described as refugees.

00:17:18.330 --> 00:17:21.180
You are familiar with that
panic in this country as well,

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:23.910
but I'll restrict my remarks to Europe

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:27.783
in order to avoid homogenizing
complicated issues,

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:31.380
which is one of the problems

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:34.023
of the representation
of people on the move.

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:39.360
It has become common to
refer to all arrivals

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:41.823
from outside of Europe as refugees,

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:47.100
when perhaps more nuanced
terms would enable us

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:50.310
to see the phenomenon of displacement

00:17:50.310 --> 00:17:51.963
and migration more clearly.

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:55.540
The use of this word

00:17:56.460 --> 00:17:59.896
in reference to the population upheavals

00:17:59.896 --> 00:18:02.140
of non-European people

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:06.430
is inflected with criminal
and malicious intention

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:10.953
and subsequent social jeopardy
for the destination country.

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:16.810
More often than not, it is
emptied of the threat to life

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:22.050
and the desperate distress
that prompted the flight

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:23.043
in the first place.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.690
Refugees are not a new
phenomenon for Europe

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:32.793
or for anywhere else in the world,

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:39.030
but for Europe which has been
convulsed in relentless wars

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:41.400
on its own soil for centuries,

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:45.633
it's even less of an unknown phenomenon.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:51.183
These movements were
not only within Europe,

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:55.353
but large ones to north and South America,

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:00.483
the victims of economic and
social distress on the move.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:05.670
Even larger numbers of
Europeans went or were sent

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:08.103
to what we now call the global south.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:14.600
These were not refugees or
they were not all refugees,

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:18.790
but people empowered
at times involuntarily

00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:22.870
by the expansionist
ideologies of their states

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:30.380
to displace and dispossess
non-European people

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:35.463
of their rights and their
lands and at times their lives,

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:39.543
and to call this process
a civilizing mission.

00:19:43.770 --> 00:19:48.770
The incredible destruction
of the 20th century wars

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:50.910
saw not only the pulverization

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:55.533
of prosperous and densely
populated cities and territories,

00:19:56.490 --> 00:20:00.183
but an unprecedented
forced transfer of people.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:07.010
What else is the refugee
but a creature on the move

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:10.203
as a result of such circumstances?

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:13.890
When we speak of such a figure,

00:20:13.890 --> 00:20:18.510
we should remind ourselves
that the European colonization

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:21.660
of the continents was also a deprivation

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:25.560
of the rights and lands of native peoples,

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:28.590
some of whom now have no recourse

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:31.350
but to seek the safety and prosperity

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:33.303
which their oppression has produced.

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:43.550
How should we begin to think
about the human obligation

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:46.203
in this phenomenon?

00:20:48.374 --> 00:20:49.207
What are we to do?

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:57.140
To me, it seems both a
moral and human obligation

00:20:58.200 --> 00:20:59.860
to offer hospitality

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:07.420
to people whose lives are at risk

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:13.790
and indeed to avoid
criminalizing a desire for safety

00:21:13.950 --> 00:21:15.303
and self-fulfillment.

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:25.050
I began by speaking about
memory as a vital resource

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:28.680
of a certain kind of writer or
for a certain kind of writer.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:30.750
And I'll return to that presently

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:33.090
in the light of the representation

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:36.573
and the self-representation
of the displaced figure.

00:21:41.438 --> 00:21:44.433
Recently in my tour of the world,

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:49.740
the reason I was asked to
speak in the same way like this

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:52.350
like I mentioned at the
beginning, will you give,

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:53.370
actually I wasn't even asked.

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:54.720
I was told.

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:56.800
You are giving the inaugural address

00:21:57.690 --> 00:22:02.250
in about two hours and
your topic is like that.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:06.520
It happens honestly, but
this time my topic was

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:10.230
writing as a form of resistance.

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:11.063
Talk about that.

00:22:11.897 --> 00:22:15.360
(audience laughing)

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:18.079
My first instinct was to resist

00:22:18.079 --> 00:22:21.079
(audience laughing)

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:25.560
because I thought this was one
of those platform statements

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:27.183
or overstatements.

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.879
Literature is the meaning of life,

00:22:30.879 --> 00:22:34.320
that kind of self-congratulation
that commentators

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:36.210
and practitioners of the writing business

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:37.593
are sometimes prone to.

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:43.680
But I had to speak to
people to whom this subject

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:47.463
had already been announced,
so there was no escape.

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:53.370
So I rephrased the topic for myself.

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:58.173
If writing is a form of resistance,
what is it resistant to?

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:05.960
In the first place I think
resistance to forgetfulness.

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:10.380
What oppressions and
tragedies befall people

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.490
at the hands of powerful forces

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:18.490
and what is known and believed
in can no longer be known

00:23:19.362 --> 00:23:20.633
and believed in in the same way,

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:23.703
the compulsion is to move on,

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:29.700
to get on with it, to forget,

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:32.070
precisely the passage you quoted,

00:23:32.070 --> 00:23:34.520
although I didn't know
you were going to do that.

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:39.810
It's a compulsion endorsed

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:42.060
by those responsible for such events.

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:44.130
Who would prefer that we do forget

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:47.403
that we do not remember what
led to our present state.

00:23:50.370 --> 00:23:53.640
A few years ago, a British Prime Minister

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:57.540
on a visit to Tanzania was
exasperated by a question

00:23:57.540 --> 00:23:58.713
about colonialism,

00:24:00.570 --> 00:24:02.407
and he was reported to reply,

00:24:02.407 --> 00:24:05.377
"Why are you talking about colonialism?

00:24:05.377 --> 00:24:07.184
"It's over.

00:24:07.184 --> 00:24:10.407
"Forget about it and let's move on."

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:15.430
It was Gordon Brown actually.

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:22.800
Our own governments would
also like us to forget

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:25.383
about their atrocities too and move on.

00:24:27.862 --> 00:24:29.970
And in many instances,
citizens have no choice

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:33.153
but to do so in order to
preserve life and decency,

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:36.153
but writers do have a choice.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:42.860
They can choose not to
allow what they know

00:24:43.020 --> 00:24:46.473
and what they remember
to pass away unnoticed.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:55.623
Writing is not only about
noble and brave ideas and acts.

00:24:56.490 --> 00:25:00.090
It is not necessarily
about fighting tyrants

00:25:00.090 --> 00:25:03.420
or necessarily about standing on platforms

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:06.483
and making powerful
speeches to energize people.

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:12.070
It's about the ordinary, mundane business

00:25:13.800 --> 00:25:16.383
of keeping alive what is important,

00:25:19.830 --> 00:25:23.130
which is about how
people imagine themselves

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:26.643
and how they see and describe
the world they live in.

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:31.920
In an important way,

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:34.473
I think there is a kind
of responsibility in that.

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:41.610
Writing is resistance to distortion

00:25:41.610 --> 00:25:45.033
by narratives that seek
to justify injustice,

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:50.880
to distract us from what our
mind should be focusing on,

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:52.470
to draw us to other things

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.140
that might appear at first more appealing

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:57.843
or more interesting or more immediate.

00:25:59.490 --> 00:26:02.373
It is resistance, in
other words, to neglect.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:08.370
And it is important to
continue to uphold those ideas

00:26:08.370 --> 00:26:11.553
and beliefs that we think are
important and that we value.

00:26:15.000 --> 00:26:17.600
You get the picture, I hope,
it's about remembering.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:23.310
A while ago I referred to the way

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:27.360
recollections of a lost time
are taken to be sentimental

00:26:27.360 --> 00:26:29.523
and suspect and nostalgia.

00:26:31.620 --> 00:26:33.753
I suggested that we hang onto that word,

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:36.990
at least in his sense of the distress

00:26:36.990 --> 00:26:39.693
aroused by yearning for
one place of origin.

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:44.502
I said earlier that it is a yearning

00:26:44.502 --> 00:26:47.202
of those who have lost their
homes know only too well.

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:54.330
Towards the end of his
memoir, "Out of Place,"

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:56.223
Edward Said says, I quote,

00:26:59.047 --> 00:27:03.000
"To this day I feel that I am,"

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:07.327
Sorry, since I'm quoting, I
want to it exactly right, huh?

00:27:07.327 --> 00:27:10.647
"To this day I still feel
that I'm away from home."

00:27:13.050 --> 00:27:14.820
I would like to dwell on that for a moment

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:18.900
on what constitutes the ongoing
memory of what has been lost

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:21.093
and how some writers have coped with that.

00:27:22.760 --> 00:27:26.210
Said has written in a variety
of ways about that experience.

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:31.833
Nuruddin Farah, the eminent Somali writer,

00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:39.270
has only ever written about
Somalia in his 11 novels,

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:42.180
even though he has not lived in Somalia

00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:44.823
for any length of time since 1970.

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:50.640
I'm concentrating mainly on writers from,

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:52.140
shall we say the global south,

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:56.883
because one can think of others
as well, Joyce, for example,

00:27:58.050 --> 00:27:59.913
Nabokov for another example.

00:28:01.573 --> 00:28:03.673
But anyway, let's stay with our community.

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:09.730
In his brilliant early
poems, Kamau Brathwaite

00:28:14.049 --> 00:28:17.460
has the figure of the
itinerant Caribbean man

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:20.977
whom he describes thus
in the poem "Folk Ways,"

00:28:20.977 --> 00:28:24.730
"Ever see a man travel
more, see more lands

00:28:25.989 --> 00:28:30.897
"than this poor landless
harborless spade?"

00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:38.640
Brathwaite poses that as a question,

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.743
ever seen anyone like him?

00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:44.350
He poses that as a question in a way,

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:46.350
and in a way it was true to his own life,

00:28:46.350 --> 00:28:50.343
which is spent largely away
from his home island Barbados.

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:53.540
But he always wrote about the Caribbean,

00:28:57.570 --> 00:29:01.057
and he returned there in his
last days to live and die.

00:29:01.057 --> 00:29:06.057
"Cow Pastor" Christ Church, Barbados.

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:08.802
The places he described, I quote,

00:29:08.802 --> 00:29:13.077
"my maroon town, resistance palenque."

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:18.723
Palenque is the ancient Mayan
city in southern Mexico,

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:24.000
and it's invocation with reference

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:26.970
to Cow Pastor, cow pasture,

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:28.170
okay, we're getting both.

00:29:28.170 --> 00:29:31.860
This is typical Brathwaite,
you know, the slash, you know?

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:33.300
It's this, it's that.

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:36.543
So it's sometimes cow pastor
sometimes it's cow pasture.

00:29:38.528 --> 00:29:41.340
But the invocation of
the reference to Palenque

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:43.950
with reference to cow pastor/cow pasture

00:29:43.950 --> 00:29:48.910
is part of Brathwaite's lifelong
idea of a new world vision

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:54.240
for the Caribbean, a
vision whose force derived

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:58.920
from an embrace and
imaginative transformation

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:02.250
of the Caribbean, the tragic
history of the Caribbean,

00:30:02.250 --> 00:30:04.713
of slavery and plantation.

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:13.260
Derek Walcott in "Schooner
Flights" has his Shabine

00:30:13.260 --> 00:30:17.700
utter the famous lines
celebrating Caribbean diversity.

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:22.700
I quote, "Either I'm
nobody or I'm a nation."

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:27.780
Before, later on, enabling that diversity

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:31.080
by casting the Caribbean
man as a Homeric hero

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:32.880
in search of his place in the world.

00:30:36.390 --> 00:30:39.390
The Guyanese novelist Wilson Harris

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:44.390
set all of his 22 novels in Guyana,

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:49.200
even though he lived most of
his adult life in Colchester

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:50.343
just outside London.

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:55.680
And of course Jean Rhys, who in her 60s

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:58.560
returned to the Dominica of her childhood

00:30:58.560 --> 00:31:00.237
to write "Wide Sargasso Sea."

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:07.180
So what is it about place
that has this incredible grip

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:10.263
on the writer's imagination?

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:14.820
What is it that makes it
so difficult to give up

00:31:14.820 --> 00:31:16.323
the force of its memory.

00:31:18.270 --> 00:31:22.083
Isn't one place as good
as another in the end?

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:28.500
The causes of the impulse to disperse,

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:33.213
to leave home often have similar sources,

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:39.050
an injustice, terror, disgust or need.

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:44.610
And while refugee appears to be a word

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:47.733
with a firm and clear
idea of its reference,

00:31:49.650 --> 00:31:53.580
it includes the terrified Rohingya child

00:31:53.580 --> 00:31:57.750
and the young African man
recklessly risking everything

00:31:57.750 --> 00:31:58.920
for a life in Europe.

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:01.770
It includes the religious persecuted

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:04.683
and the victims of war
and imperial aggression.

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:09.840
I think that what makes
it impossible to give up

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:14.840
this memory of place is
an unredeemed injustice

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:22.290
and unappeased longing

00:32:22.290 --> 00:32:26.547
where to abandon an
obligation to place and people

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:29.703
seems like treachery.

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:35.940
However, ineffectual and self tormenting

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:38.640
such memorializing might seem

00:32:38.640 --> 00:32:40.443
to a practical minded person,

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:45.780
the impulse to do so is unavoidable

00:32:45.780 --> 00:32:49.950
because to do otherwise, to forget

00:32:49.950 --> 00:32:52.473
would seem like self-indulgent neglect.

00:32:54.240 --> 00:32:55.859
Thank you very much.

00:32:55.859 --> 00:32:59.026
(audience applauding)

